atorname{ln}⁡(4x+1)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186320796 :question/problem "Express $\\frac{2x+17}{(x+5)^{2}}$ as a sum of partial fractions.\n\n|  | $\\frac{2x+17}{(x+5)^{2}}\\,=\\,\\frac{2}{(x+5)}\\,\\,+$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | $(x+5)^{2}$ |" "Express $\\frac{2x+17}{(x+5)^{2}}$ as a sum of partial fractions.\n\n|  | $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{2x+17}{(x+5)^{2}}\\,=\\,\\frac{2}{(x+5)}\\,\\,+\n$$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | $(x+5)^{2}$ |"] [:db/cas 17592186320797 :question/problem "Express $\\frac{3x+2}{(x-7)^{2}}$ as a sum of partial fractions.\n\n|  | $\\frac{3x+2}{(x-7)^{2}}\\,=\\,\\frac{3}{(x-7)}\\,\\,+$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | $(x-7)^{2}$ |" "Express $\\frac{3x+2}{(x-7)^{2}}$ as a sum of partial fractions.\n\n|  | $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{3x+2}{(x-7)^{2}}\\,=\\,\\frac{3}{(x-7)}\\,\\,+\n$$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | $(x-7)^{2}$ |"] [:db/cas 17592186320812 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{e/5}^{{e}^{4}/5}\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{e/5}^{{e}^{4}/5}\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186320813 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{3}^{81}\\frac{2}{x\\sqrt{{log}_{3}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x=($\n{{field-1}}\n$)\\operatorname{ln}⁡3$" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{3}^{81}\\frac{2}{x\\sqrt{{log}_{3}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x=(\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$)\\operatorname{ln}⁡3$"] [:db/cas 17592186320814 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{5}^{125}\\frac{8}{3x{log}_{5}⁡({x}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}x=($\n{{field-1}}\n$)\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡5$" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{5}^{125}\\frac{8}{3x{log}_{5}⁡({x}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}x=(\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$)\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡5$"] [:db/cas 17592186320815 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{1/8}\\frac{10}{\\sqrt{5-80{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1/8}\\frac{10}{\\sqrt{5-80{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186320816 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\sqrt{3}}^{\\infty }\\frac{12}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\sqrt{3}}^{\\infty }\\frac{12}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186320817 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\sqrt{3}/3}^{\\infty }\\frac{8}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\sqrt{3}/3}^{\\infty }\\frac{8}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186320818 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{8}{1+16{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{8}{1+16{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186320819 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3\\cdot {4}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3\\cdot {4}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186320821 :question/problem "Find the range of values of $k$ for which the series\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{\\left(\\frac{9}{{81}^{k}}\\right)}^{n}$\nconverges. Do not round the answer.\n\n$k$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" "Find the range of values of $k$ for which the series\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{\\left(\\frac{9}{{81}^{k}}\\right)}^{n}\n$$\nconverges. Do not round the answer.\n\n$k$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}"] [:db/cas 17592186320822 :question/problem "Find the range of values of $k$ for which the series\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{3}^{6n}}{{9}^{kn}}$\nconverges. Do not round the answer.\n\n$k$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" "Find the range of values of $k$ for which the series\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{3}^{6n}}{{9}^{kn}}\n$$\nconverges. Do not round the answer.\n\n$k$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}"] [:db/cas 17592186320822 :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the series so that it's in the form ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }a{r}^{n},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{3}^{6n}}{{9}^{kn}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{\\left(\\frac{{3}^{6}}{{9}^{k}}\\right)}^{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{\\left(\\frac{{9}^{3}}{{9}^{k}}\\right)}^{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{({9}^{3-k})}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that this is a geometric series with a common ratio of $r={9}^{3-k}.$ \n\nFor the series to converge, we require $|r|<1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{9}^{3-k}| & <1 \\\\ {9}^{3-k} & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we could remove the absolute value bars because ${9}^{3-k}$ is always positive.\n\nThe equation ${9}^{3-k}=1$ has the solution $k=3.$ Now, since ${9}^{3-k}$ decreases as $k$ increases, the solution to ${9}^{3-k}<1$ must be $k>3.$" "First, we rewrite the series so that it's in the form $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }a{r}^{n},\n$$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{3}^{6n}}{{9}^{kn}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{\\left(\\frac{{3}^{6}}{{9}^{k}}\\right)}^{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{\\left(\\frac{{9}^{3}}{{9}^{k}}\\right)}^{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{({9}^{3-k})}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that this is a geometric series with a common ratio of $r={9}^{3-k}.$ \n\nFor the series to converge, we require $|r|<1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{9}^{3-k}| & <1 \\\\ {9}^{3-k} & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we could remove the absolute value bars because ${9}^{3-k}$ is always positive.\n\nThe equation ${9}^{3-k}=1$ has the solution $k=3.$ Now, since ${9}^{3-k}$ decreases as $k$ increases, the solution to ${9}^{3-k}<1$ must be $k>3.$"] [:db/cas 17592186320831 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{2}^{3x-1}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{2}^{3x-1}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186320832 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{5}^{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{5}^{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186320833 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{3}^{2x+2}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{3}^{2x+2}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186320850 :question/worked-solution "The position of the particle at $t=7$ is equal to its position at $t=3$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [3,7].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(7)=x(3)+{\\int }_{3}^{7}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nWe are told that $x(3)=3$, and we can compute the integral ${\\int }_{3}^{7}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t$ by finding the signed area under the graph from $t=3$ to $t=7.$ Computing the signed area, we get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{3}^{7}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t=8.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nx(7)=3+8=11.\n$$\n\nSo the position of the particle at $t=7$ is $x=11.$" "The position of the particle at $t=7$ is equal to its position at $t=3$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [3,7].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(7)=x(3)+{\\int }_{3}^{7}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nWe are told that $x(3)=3$, and we can compute the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{3}^{7}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$ by finding the signed area under the graph from $t=3$ to $t=7.$ Computing the signed area, we get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{3}^{7}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t=8.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nx(7)=3+8=11.\n$$\n\nSo the position of the particle at $t=7$ is $x=11.$"] [:db/cas 17592186320852 :question/worked-solution "The position of the particle at $t=6$ is equal to its position at $t=0$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [0,6].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(6)=x(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{6}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nWe are told that $x(0)=-4$, and we can compute the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{6}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t$ by finding the signed area under the graph from $t=0$ to $t=6.$ Computing the signed area, we get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{6}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t=10.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nx(6)=-4+10=6.\n$$\n\nSo the position of the particle at $t=6$ is $x=6.$" "The position of the particle at $t=6$ is equal to its position at $t=0$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [0,6].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(6)=x(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{6}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nWe are told that $x(0)=-4$, and we can compute the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{6}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$ by finding the signed area under the graph from $t=0$ to $t=6.$ Computing the signed area, we get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{6}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t=10.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nx(6)=-4+10=6.\n$$\n\nSo the position of the particle at $t=6$ is $x=6.$"] [:db/cas 17592186320853 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\