\\,\\text{d}t=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nx(7)=-3+3=0.\n$$\n\nSo the position of the particle at $t=7$ is $x=0.$" "The position of the particle at $t=7$ is equal to its position at $t=4$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [4,7].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(7)=x(4)+{\\int }_{4}^{7}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nWe are told that $x(4)=-3$, and we can compute the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{4}^{7}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$ by finding the signed area under the graph from $t=4$ to $t=7.$ Computing the signed area, we get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{4}^{7}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nx(7)=-3+3=0.\n$$\n\nSo the position of the particle at $t=7$ is $x=0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186320856 :question/worked-solution "The position of the particle at $t=7$ is equal to its position at $t=2$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [2,7].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(7)=x(2)+{\\int }_{2}^{7}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nWe are told that $x(2)=8$, and we can compute the integral ${\\int }_{2}^{7}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t$ by finding the signed area under the graph from $t=2$ to $t=7.$ Computing the signed area, we get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{7}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t=-3.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nx(7)=8+(-3)=5.\n$$\n\nSo the position of the particle at $t=7$ is $x=5.$" "The position of the particle at $t=7$ is equal to its position at $t=2$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [2,7].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(7)=x(2)+{\\int }_{2}^{7}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nWe are told that $x(2)=8$, and we can compute the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{2}^{7}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$ by finding the signed area under the graph from $t=2$ to $t=7.$ Computing the signed area, we get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{7}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t=-3.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nx(7)=8+(-3)=5.\n$$\n\nSo the position of the particle at $t=7$ is $x=5.$"] [:db/cas 17592186320859 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}