\nFinally, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1/3}^{e/3}243{x}^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{2}\\text{d}x & ={[{x}^{3}(81{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))}^{2}-54\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)+18)]}_{1/3}^{e/3} \\\\  & =[{\\left(\\frac{e}{3}\\right)}^{3}(81{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{e}{3}))}^{2}-54\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{e}{3})+18)] \\\\  & \\,-[{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{3}(81{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}))}^{2}-54\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{1}{3})+18)] \\\\  & =[\\frac{{e}^{3}}{27}(81{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡e)}^{2}-54\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+18)]-[\\frac{1}{27}(81{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)}^{2}-54\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+18)] \\\\  & =[\\frac{{e}^{3}}{27}(81-54+18)]-\\frac{1}{27}\\cdot 18 \\\\  & =\\frac{5{e}^{3}-2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186320947 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{e-1}27(x+1)^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{e-1}27(x+1)^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186320947 :question/worked-solution "We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1))^{2} & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)}{x+1}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=27(x+1)^{2} & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int 27(x+1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=9(x+1)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 27(x+1)^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1))}^{2}(9(x+1)^{3})-\\int (9(x+1)^{3})\\left(\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)}{x+1}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =9(x+1)^{3}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1))}^{2}-18\\int (x+1)^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $\\int (x+1)^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)\\,\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1) & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x+1}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=(x+1)^{2} & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int (x+1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{(x+1)^{3}}{3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (x+1)^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)\\left(\\frac{(x+1)^{3}}{3}\\right)-\\int \\left(\\frac{(x+1)^{3}}{3}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{x+1}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}\\int (x+1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)}{3}-\\frac{(x+1)^{3}}{9}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 27(x+1)^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =9(x+1)^{3}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1))}^{2}-18\\int (x+1)^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =9(x+1)^{3}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1))}^{2}-18(\\frac{(x+1)^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)}{3}-\\frac{(x+1)^{3}}{9}+D) \\\\  & =9(x+1)^{3}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1))}^{2}-6(x+1)^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+2(x+1)^{3}-18D \\\\  & =(x+1)^{3}(9{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1))}^{2}-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+2)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-18D.$\n\nFinally, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{e-1}27(x+1)^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1))^{2}\\text{d}x & ={[(x+1)^{3}(9{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1))}^{2}-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+2)]}_{0}^{e-1} \\\\  & =[{e}^{3}(9{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡e)}^{2}-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+2)]-[{1}^{3}(9{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)}^{2}-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+2)] \\\\  & =[{e}^{3}(9-6+2)]-2 \\\\  & =5{e}^{3}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1))^{2} & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)}{x+1}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=27(x+1)^{2} & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int 27(x+1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=9(x+1)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 27(x+1)^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1))}^{2}(9(x+1)^{3})-\\int (9(x+1)^{3})\\left(\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)}{x+1}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =9(x+1)^{3}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1))}^{2}-18\\int (x+1)^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int (x+1)^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1) & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x+1}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=(x+1)^{2} & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int (x+1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{(x+1)^{3}}{3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (x+1)^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)\\left(\\frac{(x+1)^{3}}{3}\\right)-\\int \\left(\\frac{(x+1)^{3}}{3}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{x+1}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}\\int (x+1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)}{3}-\\frac{(x+1)^{3}}{9}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 27(x+1)^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =9(x+1)^{3}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1))}^{2}-18\\int (x+1)^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =9(x+1)^{3}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1))}^{2}-18(\\frac{(x+1)^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)}{3}-\\frac{(x+1)^{3}}{9}+D) \\\\  & =9(x+1)^{3}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1))}^{2}-6(x+1)^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+2(x+1)^{3}-18D \\\\  & =(x+1)^{3}(9{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1))}^{2}-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+2)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-18D.$\n\nFinally, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{e-1}27(x+1)^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1))^{2}\\text{d}x & ={[(x+1)^{3}(9{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1))}^{2}-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+2)]}_{0}^{e-1} \\\\  & =[{e}^{3}(9{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡e)}^{2}-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+2)]-[{1}^{3}(9{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)}^{2}-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+2)] \\\\  & =[{e}^{3}(9-6+2)]-2 \\\\  & =5{e}^{3}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186320952 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+5){e}^{2x+2}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+5){e}^{2x+2}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186320952 :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={x}^{2}+5\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{2x+2}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {e}^{2x+2}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{e}^{2x+2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({x}^{2}+5){e}^{2x+2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+5)\\left(\\frac{{e}^{2x+2}}{2}\\right)-\\int \\left(\\frac{{e}^{2x+2}}{2}\\right)(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+5){e}^{2x+2}}{2}-\\int x\\,{e}^{2x+2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $\\int x\\,{e}^{2x+2}\\,\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{2x+2}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {e}^{2x+2}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{e}^{2x+2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\,{e}^{2x+2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(x)\\left(\\frac{{e}^{2x+2}}{2}\\right)-\\int \\left(\\frac{{e}^{2x+2}}{2}\\right)(1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{x\\,{e}^{2x+2}}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{2x+2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{x\\,{e}^{2x+2}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{2x+2}}{4}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({x}^{2}+5){e}^{2x+2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+5){e}^{2x+2}}{2}-\\int x\\,{e}^{2x+2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+5){e}^{2x+2}}{2}-(\\frac{x\\,{e}^{2x+2}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{2x+2}}{4}+D) \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+5){e}^{2x+2}}{2}-\\frac{x\\,{e}^{2x+2}}{2}+\\frac{{e}^{2x+2}}{4}-D \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2x+2}}{4}(2{x}^{2}-2x+11)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-D.$ \n\nFinally then, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+5){e}^{2x+2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={[\\frac{{e}^{2x+2}}{4}(2{x}^{2}-2x+11)]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{e}^{2(1)+2}}{4}(2(1)^{2}-2(1)+11))-(\\frac{{e}^{2(0)+2}}{4}(2(0)^{2}-2(0)+11)) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{4}}{4}(2-2+11)-\\frac{{e}^{2}}{4}(0-0+11) \\\\  & =\\frac{11{e}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{11{e}^{2}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{11{e}^{4}-11{e}^{2}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={x}^{2}+5\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{2x+2}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {e}^{2x+2}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{e}^{2x+2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({x}^{2}+5){e}^{2x+2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+5)\\left(\\frac{{e}^{2x+2}}{2}\\right)-\\int \\left(\\frac{{e}^{2x+2}}{2}\\right)(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+5){e}^{2x+2}}{2}-\\int x\\,{e}^{2x+2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int x\\,{e}^{2x+2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{2x+2}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {e}^{2x+2}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{e}^{2x+2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\,{e}^{2x+2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(x)\\left(\\frac{{e}^{2x+2}}{2}\\right)-\\int \\left(\\frac{{e}^{2x+2}}{2}\\right)(1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{x\\,{e}^{2x+2}}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{2x+2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{x\\,{e}^{2x+2}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{2x+2}}{4}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({x}^{2}+5){e}^{2x+2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+5){e}^{2x+2}}{2}-\\int x\\,{e}^{2x+2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+5){e}^{2x+2}}{2}-(\\frac{x\\,{e}^{2x+2}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{2x+2}}{4}+D) \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+5){e}^{2x+2}}{2}-\\frac{x\\,{e}^{2x+2}}{2}+\\frac{{e}^{2x+2}}{4}-D \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2x+2}}{4}(2{x}^{2}-2x+11)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-D.$ \n\nFinally then, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+5){e}^{2x+2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={[\\frac{{e}^{2x+2}}{4}(2{x}^{2}-2x+11)]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{e}^{2(1)+2}}{4}(2(1)^{2}-2(1)+11))-(\\frac{{e}^{2(0)+2}}{4}(2(0)^{2}-2(0)+11)) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{4}}{4}(2-2+11)-\\frac{{e}^{2}}{4}(0-0+11) \\\\  & =\\frac{11{e}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{11{e}^{2}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{11{e}^{4}-11{e}^{2}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186320953 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+6){e}^{2x-1}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+6){e}^{2x-1}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186320953 :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={x}^{2}+6\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{2x-1}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {e}^{2x-1}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{e}^{2x-1}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({x}^{2}+6){e}^{2x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+6)\\left(\\frac{{e}^{2x-1}}{2}\\right)-\\int \\left(\\frac{{e}^{2x-1}}{2}\\right)(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+6){e}^{2x-1}}{2}-\\int x\\,{e}^{2x-1}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $\\int x\\,{e}^{2x-1}\\,\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{2x-1}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {e}^{2x-1}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{e}^{2x-1}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\,{e}^{2x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(x)\\left(\\frac{{e}^{2x-1}}{2}\\right)-\\int \\left(\\frac{{e}^{2x-1}}{2}\\right)(1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{x\\,{e}^{2x-1}}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{2x-1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{x\\,{e}^{2x-1}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{2x-1}}{4}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({x}^{2}+6){e}^{2x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+6){e}^{2x-1}}{2}-\\int x\\,{e}^{2x-1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+6){e}^{2x-1}}{2}-(\\frac{x\\,{e}^{2x-1}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{2x-1}}{4}+D) \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+6){e}^{2x-1}}{2}-\\frac{x\\,{e}^{2x-1}}{2}+\\frac{{e}^{2x-1}}{4}-D \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2x-1}}{4}(2{x}^{2}-2x+13)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-D.$ \n\nFinally then, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+6){e}^{2x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={[\\frac{{e}^{2x-1}}{4}(2{x}^{2}-2x+13)]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{e}^{2(1)-1}}{4}(2(1)^{2}-2(1)+13))-(\\frac{{e}^{2(0)-1}}{4}(2(0)^{2}-2(0)+13)) \\\\  & =\\frac{e}{4}(2-2+13)-\\frac{1}{4e}(0-0+13) \\\\  & =\\frac{13e}{4}-\\frac{13}{4e} \\\\  & =\\frac{13{e}^{2}-13}{4e}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={x}^{2}+6\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{2x-1}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {e}^{2x-1}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{e}^{2x-1}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({x}^{2}+6){e}^{2x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+6)\\left(\\frac{{e}^{2x-1}}{2}\\right)-\\int \\left(\\frac{{e}^{2x-1}}{2}\\right)(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+6){e}^{2x-1}}{2}-\\int x\\,{e}^{2x-1}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int x\\,{e}^{2x-1}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{2x-1}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {e}^{2x-1}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{e}^{2x-1}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\,{e}^{2x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(x)\\left(\\frac{{e}^{2x-1}}{2}\\right)-\\int \\left(\\frac{{e}^{2x-1}}{2}\\right)(1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{x\\,{e}^{2x-1}}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{2x-1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{x\\,{e}^{2x-1}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{2x-1}}{4}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({x}^{2}+6){e}^{2x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+6){e}^{2x-1}}{2}-\\int x\\,{e}^{2x-1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+6){e}^{2x-1}}{2}-(\\frac{x\\,{e}^{2x-1}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{2x-1}}{4}+D) \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+6){e}^{2x-1}}{2}-\\frac{x\\,{e}^{2x-1}}{2}+\\frac{{e}^{2x-1}}{4}-D \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2x-1}}{4}(2{x}^{2}-2x+13)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-D.$ \n\nFinally then, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+6){e}^{2x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={[\\frac{{e}^{2x-1}}{4}(2{x}^{2}-2x+13)]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{e}^{2(1)-1}}{4}(2(1)^{2}-2(1)+13))-(\\frac{{e}^{2(0)-1}}{4}(2(0)^{2}-2(0)+13)) \\\\  & =\\frac{e}{4}(2-2+13)-\\frac{1}{4e}(0-0+13) \\\\  & =\\frac{13e}{4}-\\frac{13}{4e} \\\\  & =\\frac{13{e}^{2}-13}{4e}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186320954 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}4{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}4{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186320954 :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=4{t}^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}=8t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 4{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(4{t}^{2})(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)(8t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-4{t}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+8\\int t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we use integration by parts to evaluate $\\int t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=t & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}=1\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\text{d}t=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,dt \\\\  & =t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 4{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =-4{t}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+8\\int t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-4{t}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+8(t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+D) \\\\  & =-4{t}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+8t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=8D.$\n\nFinally, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}4{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & ={[-4{t}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+8t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =(0+8\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)(1)+0)-(0+0+8) \\\\  & =4\\pi -8 \\\\  & =4\\pi -8\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=4{t}^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}=8t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 4{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(4{t}^{2})(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)(8t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-4{t}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+8\\int t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we use integration by parts to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=t & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}=1\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\text{d}t=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,dt \\\\  & =t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 4{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =-4{t}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+8\\int t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-4{t}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+8(t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+D) \\\\  & =-4{t}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+8t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=8D.$\n\nFinally, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}4{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & ={[-4{t}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+8t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =(0+8\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)(1)+0)-(0+0+8) \\\\  & =4\\pi -8 \\\\  & =4\\pi -8\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186320955 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186320955 :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=3{x}^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=6x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(3{x}^{2})(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))-\\int (\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))(6x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)-6\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we use integration by parts to evaluate $\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))\\cdot 1\\,dx \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,dx \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)-6\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)-6(-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+D) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+6x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-6D.$\n\nFinally, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={[3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+6x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)]}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =(3(\\pi )^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )+6(\\pi )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ))-(3(0)^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+6(0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =0+6\\pi (-1)-0-0 \\\\  & =-6\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=3{x}^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=6x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(3{x}^{2})(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))-\\int (\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))(6x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)-6\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we use integration by parts to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))\\cdot 1\\,dx \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,dx \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)-6\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)-6(-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+D) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+6x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-6D.$\n\nFinally, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={[3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+6x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)]}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =(3(\\pi )^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )+6(\\pi )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ))-(3(0)^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+6(0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =0+6\\pi (-1)-0-0 \\\\  & =-6\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186320956 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{e}81{x}^{2}({log}_{4}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{{ln}^{2}⁡4}(${{field-1}}$)$" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{e}81{x}^{2}({log}_{4}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{{ln}^{2}⁡4}(\n$${{field-1}}$)$"] [:db/cas 17592186320956 :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=({log}_{4}⁡x)^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2{log}_{4}⁡x}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡4} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=81{x}^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int 81{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=27{x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 81{x}^{2}({log}_{4}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={({log}_{4}⁡x)}^{2}(27{x}^{3})-\\int (27{x}^{3})\\left(\\frac{2{log}_{4}⁡x}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =27{x}^{3}{({log}_{4}⁡x)}^{2}-\\frac{54}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\int {x}^{2}{log}_{4}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate  $\\int {x}^{2}{log}_{4}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={log}_{4}⁡x\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡4} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={x}^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}{log}_{4}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({log}_{4}⁡x)\\left(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}\\right)-\\int \\left(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}{log}_{4}⁡x}{3}-\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}{log}_{4}⁡x}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{9\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant.\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 81{x}^{2}({log}_{4}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =27{x}^{3}{({log}_{4}⁡x)}^{2}-\\frac{54}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}(\\frac{{x}^{3}{log}_{4}⁡x}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{9\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}+D) \\\\  & =27{x}^{3}{({log}_{4}⁡x)}^{2}-\\frac{18{x}^{3}{log}_{4}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}+\\frac{6{x}^{3}}{{ln}^{2}⁡4}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{27{x}^{3}{ln}^{2}⁡x}{{ln}^{2}⁡4}-\\frac{18{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{ln}^{2}⁡4}+\\frac{6{x}^{3}}{{ln}^{2}⁡4}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{ln}^{2}⁡4}(27{ln}^{2}⁡x-18\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+6)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-\\frac{54D}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}.$\n\nNote that in the above calculation, we used the change of base formula ${log}_{4}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}.$\n\nFinally, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}81{x}^{2}({log}_{4}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{ln}^{2}⁡4}(27{ln}^{2}⁡x-18\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+6)]}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3}}{{ln}^{2}⁡4}(27{ln}^{2}⁡e-18\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+6)-\\frac{{1}^{3}}{{ln}^{2}⁡4}(27{ln}^{2}⁡1-18\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+6) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3}}{{ln}^{2}⁡4}(27-18+6)-\\frac{1}{{ln}^{2}⁡4}(0-0+6) \\\\  & =\\frac{15{e}^{3}}{{ln}^{2}⁡4}-\\frac{6}{{ln}^{2}⁡4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{ln}^{2}⁡4}(\\,15{e}^{3}-6\\,)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=({log}_{4}⁡x)^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2{log}_{4}⁡x}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡4} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=81{x}^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int 81{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=27{x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 81{x}^{2}({log}_{4}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={({log}_{4}⁡x)}^{2}(27{x}^{3})-\\int (27{x}^{3})\\left(\\frac{2{log}_{4}⁡x}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =27{x}^{3}{({log}_{4}⁡x)}^{2}-\\frac{54}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\int {x}^{2}{log}_{4}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate  $$\n\\displaystyle \\int {x}^{2}{log}_{4}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={log}_{4}⁡x\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡4} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={x}^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}{log}_{4}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({log}_{4}⁡x)\\left(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}\\right)-\\int \\left(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}{log}_{4}⁡x}{3}-\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}{log}_{4}⁡x}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{9\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant.\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 81{x}^{2}({log}_{4}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =27{x}^{3}{({log}_{4}⁡x)}^{2}-\\frac{54}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}(\\frac{{x}^{3}{log}_{4}⁡x}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{9\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}+D) \\\\  & =27{x}^{3}{({log}_{4}⁡x)}^{2}-\\frac{18{x}^{3}{log}_{4}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}+\\frac{6{x}^{3}}{{ln}^{2}⁡4}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{27{x}^{3}{ln}^{2}⁡x}{{ln}^{2}⁡4}-\\frac{18{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{ln}^{2}⁡4}+\\frac{6{x}^{3}}{{ln}^{2}⁡4}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{ln}^{2}⁡4}(27{ln}^{2}⁡x-18\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+6)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-\\frac{54D}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}.$\n\nNote that in the above calculation, we used the change of base formula ${log}_{4}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}.$\n\nFinally, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}81{x}^{2}({log}_{4}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{ln}^{2}⁡4}(27{ln}^{2}⁡x-18\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+6)]}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3}}{{ln}^{2}⁡4}(27{ln}^{2}⁡e-18\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+6)-\\frac{{1}^{3}}{{ln}^{2}⁡4}(27{ln}^{2}⁡1-18\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+6) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3}}{{ln}^{2}⁡4}(27-18+6)-\\frac{1}{{ln}^{2}⁡4}(0-0+6) \\\\  & =\\frac{15{e}^{3}}{{ln}^{2}⁡4}-\\frac{6}{{ln}^{2}⁡4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{ln}^{2}⁡4}(\\,15{e}^{3}-6\\,)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186320957 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{e}({log}_{4}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{{ln}^{2}⁡(4)}(${{field-1}}$)$" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{e}({log}_{4}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{{ln}^{2}⁡(4)}(\n$${{field-1}}$)$"] [:db/cas 17592186320957 :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=({log}_{4}⁡x)^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2{log}_{4}⁡x}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡4} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=1\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x=x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({log}_{4}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={({log}_{4}⁡x)}^{2}(x)-\\int (x)\\left(\\frac{2{log}_{4}⁡x}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{({log}_{4}⁡x)}^{2}-\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\int {log}_{4}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate  $\\int {log}_{4}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={log}_{4}⁡x\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡4} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=1\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x=x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {log}_{4}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({log}_{4}⁡x)(x)-\\int (x)\\left(\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{log}_{4}⁡x-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{log}_{4}⁡x-\\frac{x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant.\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({log}_{4}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =x{({log}_{4}⁡x)}^{2}-\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}(x{log}_{4}⁡x-\\frac{x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}+D) \\\\  & =x{({log}_{4}⁡x)}^{2}-\\frac{2x{log}_{4}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}+\\frac{2x}{{ln}^{2}⁡4}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{x{ln}^{2}⁡x}{{ln}^{2}⁡4}-\\frac{2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{ln}^{2}⁡4}+\\frac{2x}{{ln}^{2}⁡4}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{{ln}^{2}⁡4}({ln}^{2}⁡x-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+2)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-\\frac{2D}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}.$\n\nNote that in the above calculation we used the change of base formula ${log}_{4}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}.$\n\nFinally, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}({log}_{4}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={[\\frac{x}{{ln}^{2}⁡4}({ln}^{2}⁡x-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+2)]}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =\\frac{e}{{ln}^{2}⁡4}({ln}^{2}⁡e-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+2)-\\frac{1}{{ln}^{2}⁡4}({ln}^{2}⁡1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{e}{{ln}^{2}⁡4}(1-2+2)-\\frac{1}{{ln}^{2}⁡4}(0-0+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{e}{{ln}^{2}⁡4}-\\frac{2}{{ln}^{2}⁡4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{ln}^{2}⁡4}(\\,e-2\\,)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=({log}_{4}⁡x)^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2{log}_{4}⁡x}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡4} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=1\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x=x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({log}_{4}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={({log}_{4}⁡x)}^{2}(x)-\\int (x)\\left(\\frac{2{log}_{4}⁡x}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{({log}_{4}⁡x)}^{2}-\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\int {log}_{4}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate  $$\n\\displaystyle \\int {log}_{4}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={log}_{4}⁡x\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡4} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=1\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x=x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {log}_{4}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({log}_{4}⁡x)(x)-\\int (x)\\left(\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{log}_{4}⁡x-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{log}_{4}⁡x-\\frac{x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant.\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({log}_{4}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =x{({log}_{4}⁡x)}^{2}-\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}(x{log}_{4}⁡x-\\frac{x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}+D) \\\\  & =x{({log}_{4}⁡x)}^{2}-\\frac{2x{log}_{4}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}+\\frac{2x}{{ln}^{2}⁡4}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{x{ln}^{2}⁡x}{{ln}^{2}⁡4}-\\frac{2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{ln}^{2}⁡4}+\\frac{2x}{{ln}^{2}⁡4}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{{ln}^{2}⁡4}({ln}^{2}⁡x-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+2)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-\\frac{2D}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}.$\n\nNote that in the above calculation we used the change of base formula ${log}_{4}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}.$\n\nFinally, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}({log}_{4}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={[\\frac{x}{{ln}^{2}⁡4}({ln}^{2}⁡x-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+2)]}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =\\frac{e}{{ln}^{2}⁡4}({ln}^{2}⁡e-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+2)-\\frac{1}{{ln}^{2}⁡4}({ln}^{2}⁡1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{e}{{ln}^{2}⁡4}(1-2+2)-\\frac{1}{{ln}^{2}⁡4}(0-0+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{e}{{ln}^{2}⁡4}-\\frac{2}{{ln}^{2}⁡4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{ln}^{2}⁡4}(\\,e-2\\,)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186320958 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1/2}^{e/2}2({log}_{3}⁡(2x))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{{ln}^{2}⁡(3)}(${{field-1}}$)$" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1/2}^{e/2}2({log}_{3}⁡(2x))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{{ln}^{2}⁡(3)}(\n$${{field-1}}$)$"] [:db/cas 17592186320958 :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=({log}_{3}⁡(2x))^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2{log}_{3}⁡(2x)}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=2\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int 2\\,\\text{d}x=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2({log}_{3}⁡(2x))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={({log}_{3}⁡(2x))}^{2}(2x)-\\int (2x)\\left(\\frac{2{log}_{3}⁡(2x)}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2x{({log}_{3}⁡(2x))}^{2}-\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\int {log}_{3}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate  $\\int {log}_{3}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={log}_{3}⁡(2x)\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=1\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x=x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {log}_{3}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({log}_{3}⁡(2x))(x)-\\int (x)\\left(\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{log}_{3}⁡(2x)-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{log}_{3}⁡(2x)-\\frac{x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant.\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2({log}_{3}⁡(2x))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =2x{({log}_{3}⁡(2x))}^{2}-\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}(x{log}_{3}⁡(2x)-\\frac{x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+D) \\\\  & =2x{({log}_{3}⁡(2x))}^{2}-\\frac{4x{log}_{3}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+\\frac{4x}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2x{ln}^{2}⁡(2x)}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}-\\frac{4x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}+\\frac{4x}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}(2{ln}^{2}⁡(2x)-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)+4)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-\\frac{4D}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}.$\n\nNote that in the above calculation we used the change of base formula ${log}_{3}⁡(2x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}.$\n\nFinally, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1/2}^{e/2}2({log}_{3}⁡(2x))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={[\\frac{x}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}(2{ln}^{2}⁡(2x)-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)+4)]}_{1/2}^{e/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{e/2}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}(2{ln}^{2}⁡e-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+4)-\\frac{1/2}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}(2{ln}^{2}⁡1-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+4) \\\\  & =\\frac{e/2}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}(2-4+4)-\\frac{1/2}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}(0-0+4) \\\\  & =\\frac{e}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}-\\frac{2}{{ln}^{2}⁡3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}(\\,e-2\\,)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=({log}_{3}⁡(2x))^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2{log}_{3}⁡(2x)}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=2\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int 2\\,\\text{d}x=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2({log}_{3}⁡(2x))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={({log}_{3}⁡(2x))}^{2}(2x)-\\int (2x)\\left(\\frac{2{log}_{3}⁡(2x)}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2x{({log}_{3}⁡(2x))}^{2}-\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\int {log}_{3}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate  $$\n\\displaystyle \\int {log}_{3}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={log}_{3}⁡(2x)\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=1\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x=x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {log}_{3}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({log}_{3}⁡(2x))(x)-\\int (x)\\left(\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{log}_{3}⁡(2x)-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{log}_{3}⁡(2x)-\\frac{x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant.\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2({log}_{3}⁡(2x))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =2x{({log}_{3}⁡(2x))}^{2}-\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}(x{log}_{3}⁡(2x)-\\frac{x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+D) \\\\  & =2x{({log}_{3}⁡(2x))}^{2}-\\frac{4x{log}_{3}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+\\frac{4x}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2x{ln}^{2}⁡(2x)}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}-\\frac{4x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}+\\frac{4x}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}(2{ln}^{2}⁡(2x)-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)+4)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-\\frac{4D}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}.$\n\nNote that in the above calculation we used the change of base formula ${log}_{3}⁡(2x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}.$\n\nFinally, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1/2}^{e/2}2({log}_{3}⁡(2x))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={[\\frac{x}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}(2{ln}^{2}⁡(2x)-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)+4)]}_{1/2}^{e/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{e/2}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}(2{ln}^{2}⁡e-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+4)-\\frac{1/2}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}(2{ln}^{2}⁡1-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+4) \\\\  & =\\frac{e/2}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}(2-4+4)-\\frac{1/2}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}(0-0+4) \\\\  & =\\frac{e}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}-\\frac{2}{{ln}^{2}⁡3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}(\\,e-2\\,)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186320961 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral:\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{7x+4}{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x=$ \n{{field-1}} $-\\,3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(${{field-2}}$)$" "Evaluate the following integral:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{7x+4}{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$ \n{{field-1}} $-\\,3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(${{field-2}}$)$"] [:db/cas 17592186320962 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral:\n\n${\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{8x+1}{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x=$ \n{{field-1}} $-\\,23\\operatorname{ln}⁡(${{field-2}}$)$" "Evaluate the following integral:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{8x+1}{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$ \n{{field-1}} $-\\,23\\operatornam