}_{t\\,=\\,1}=\n$$ \n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186320983 :question/problem "A curve has parametric equations $x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),\\,y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-1.$ Given that $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3t),$ determine $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{9}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,{|}_{t\\,=\\,\\pi /9}=$\n{{field-1}}" "A curve has parametric equations $x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),\\,y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-1.$ Given that $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3t),$ determine $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{9}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,{|}_{t\\,=\\,\\pi /9}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186320985 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral:\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{8x-7}{5x+1}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}} $-\\,\\frac{43}{25}\\operatorname{ln}⁡($\n{{field-2}} $)$" "Evaluate the following integral:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{8x-7}{5x+1}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}} $-\\,\\frac{43}{25}\\operatorname{ln}⁡($\n{{field-2}} $)$"] [:db/cas 17592186320988 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\pi /15}^{0}8\\operatorname{sec}⁡5x(\\operatorname{tan}⁡5x-{cos}^{2}⁡5x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\pi /15}^{0}8\\operatorname{sec}⁡5x(\\operatorname{tan}⁡5x-{cos}^{2}⁡5x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186320991 :question/problem "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=0$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{x}^{n}}{(2n+1)!}.\n$$\n\nFind the series expansion of ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }$\n{{field-1}}\n$(-1)^{n+1}{x}^{n-1}$" "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=0$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{x}^{n}}{(2n+1)!}.\n$$\n\nFind the series expansion of ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {f}^{′}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$(-1)^{n+1}{x}^{n-1}$"] [:db/cas 17592186320992 :question/problem "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=0$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2\\,{x}^{n+1}}{(n+1)!}.\n$$\n\nFind the series expansion of ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }$\n{{field-1}}\n${x}^{n}$" "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=0$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2\\,{x}^{n+1}}{(n+1)!}.\n$$\n\nFind the series expansion of ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {f}^{′}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n${x}^{n}$"] [:db/cas 17592186321013 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{1-{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{1-{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186321015 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{{tan}^{2}⁡x}{{sec}^{3}⁡x-\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{{tan}^{2}⁡x}{{sec}^{3}⁡x-\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186321020 :question/problem "Given that the trace of the elliptic cone $\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}={z}^{2}$ and the $xz$-plane are two lines, what is the intersection point of these lines?" "Given that the trace of the elliptic cone $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}={z}^{2}\n$$ and the $xz$-plane are two lines, what is the intersection point of these lines?"] [:db/cas 17592186321024 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{6(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{6(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186321025 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}4({sec}^{2}⁡x-{tan}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}4({sec}^{2}⁡x-{tan}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186321026 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}(1+{cot}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}(1+{cot}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186321027 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186321027 :question/worked-solution "We use the identity $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x. \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-(\\frac{1}{2}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We use the identity $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x},\n$$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x. \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-(\\frac{1}{2}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186321028 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}(2-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}(2-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186321028 :question/worked-solution "We use the identity $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}$ and $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}(2-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x(2-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =(2\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})-(0+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We use the identity $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x},\n$$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}(2-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x(2-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =(2\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})-(0+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186321029 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{\\pi /2}({cot}^{2}⁡x-{csc}^{2}⁡x+2x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{\\pi /2}({cot}^{2}⁡x-{csc}^{2}⁡x+2x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186321030 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}(3-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}(3-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186321030 :question/worked-solution "We use the identity $\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}(3-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x(3-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =(3\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})-(0+1) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We use the identity $\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x},\n$$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}(3-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x(3-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =(3\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})-(0+1) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186321031 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{1-{cos}^{2}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{4})}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{1-{cos}^{2}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{4})}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186321035 :question/problem "If \n$H=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 4 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -5 & -7 & -6 \\\\ 6 & 0 & 12 & 15 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 5 & 7 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 0 & -8 & -10 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$, find $\\text{rank}(H).$" "If \n$$\n\\displaystyle H=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 4 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -5 & -7 & -6 \\\\ 6 & 0 & 12 & 15 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 5 & 7 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 0 & -8 & -10 & -4\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ find $\\text{rank}(H).$"] [:db/cas 17592186321036 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}\\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}\\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186321037 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}[4{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}-1)+3{x}^{2}+4{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}-1)]\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}[4{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}-1)+3{x}^{2}+4{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}-1)]\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186321038 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi /3}\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{3})}{1+{tan}^{2}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{3})}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi /3}\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{3})}{1+{tan}^{2}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{3})}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186321039 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})}{1+{cot}^{2}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})}{1+{cot}^{2}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186321040 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}{sec}^{3}\\,x\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}{sec}^{3}\\,x\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186321041 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /12}\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /12}\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186321042 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186321044 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{1-{tan}^{2}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{1-{tan}^{2}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186321044 :question/worked-solution "We use the identities $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}$  and  $\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{1-{tan}^{2}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}(1-{tan}^{2}⁡x)\\cdot \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}(1-\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x})\\cdot \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\left(\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}{sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡x{|}_{0}^{\\pi /6} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)-\\operatorname{tan}⁡0 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We use the identities $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\n$$  and  $\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{1-{tan}^{2}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}(1-{tan}^{2}⁡x)\\cdot \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}(1-\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x})\\cdot \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\left(\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}{sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡x{|}_{0}^{\\pi /6} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)-\\operatorname{tan}⁡0 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186321045 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{1+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{1+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186321045 :question/worked-solution "We use the identities $\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$  and  $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{1+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}\\cdot (1+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x(1+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x){|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}-\\frac{1}{2})-(0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We use the identities $\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$  and  $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x},\n$$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{1+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}\\cdot (1+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x(1+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x){|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}-\\frac{1}{2})-(0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186321046 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡4x}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡4x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡4x}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡4x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186321046 :question/worked-solution "We can write the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡4x}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡4x}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\operatorname{cot}⁡4x\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡4x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThen, we use the fact that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡4x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x}$ and $\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡4x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=4x.$ Then $\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u.$ \n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=4x.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ |\n\nTherefore, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(sin\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-sin\\,(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We can write the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡4x}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡4x}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\operatorname{cot}⁡4x\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡4x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThen, we use the fact that $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{cot}⁡4x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x}\n$$ and $\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡4x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=4x.$ Then $\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u.$ \n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=4x.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ |\n\nTherefore, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(sin\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-sin\\,(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186321047 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /10}\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡5x}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡5x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /10}\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡5x}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡5x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186321047 :question/worked-solution "We can rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /10}\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡5x}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡5x}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /10}\\operatorname{cot}⁡5x\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡5x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThen, we use the fact that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡5x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x}$ and $\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡5x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /10}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡5x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=5x.$ Then $\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{5}\\text{d}u.$\n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=5x.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{10}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ |\n\nTherefore, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(sin\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We can rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /10}\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡5x}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡5x}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /10}\\operatorname{cot}⁡5x\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡5x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThen, we use the fact that $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{cot}⁡5x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x}\n$$ and $\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡5x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /10}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡5x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=5x.$ Then $\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{5}\\text{d}u.$\n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=5x.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{10}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ |\n\nTherefore, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(sin\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186321048 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /12}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡4x}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡4x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /12}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡4x}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡4x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186321048 :question/worked-solution "We can rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /12}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡4x}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡4x}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /12}\\operatorname{tan}⁡4x\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡4x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThen, we use the fact that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡4x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x}$ and $\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡4x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /12}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /12}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=4x.$ Then $\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u.$\n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=4x.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ |\n\nTherefore, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /12}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u{|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}(cos\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{1}{2}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We can rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /12}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡4x}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡4x}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /12}\\operatorname{tan}⁡4x\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡4x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThen, we use the fact that $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{tan}⁡4x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x}\n$$ and $\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡4x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /12}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /12}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=4x.$ Then $\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u.$\n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=4x.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ |\n\nTherefore, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /12}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u{|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}(cos\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{1}{2}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186321050 :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{8}+\\frac{1}{4{x}^{2}},$ $a=1,$ and $b=2.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1+{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3}}]}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{6}-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{4{x}^{6}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{6}+\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{4{x}^{6}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3}})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3}}$ to the formula for the arc length, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{4}-\\frac{1}{4{x}^{2}})|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{8}(2)^{4}-\\frac{1}{4(2)^{2}}]-[\\frac{1}{8}(1)^{4}-\\frac{1}{4(1)^{2}}] \\\\  & =\\frac{31}{16}-\\left(-\\frac{1}{8}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{33}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{8}+\\frac{1}{4{x}^{2}},\n$$ $a=1,$ and $b=2.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1+{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3}}]}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{6}-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{4{x}^{6}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{6}+\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{4{x}^{6}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3}})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $$\n\\displaystyle \\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3}}\n$$ to the formula for the arc length, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{4}-\\frac{1}{4{x}^{2}})|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{8}(2)^{4}-\\frac{1}{4(2)^{2}}]-[\\frac{1}{8}(1)^{4}-\\frac{1}{4(1)^{2}}] \\\\  & =\\frac{31}{16}-\\left(-\\frac{1}{8}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{33}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186321052 :question/problem "Given that $I(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}-{e}^{-t})^{3}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${I}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${I}^{′}(t)=3{e}^{-t}(\\,$\n{{field-1}}$)$" "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle I(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}-{e}^{-t})^{3}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$ calculate ${I}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${I}^{′}(t)=3{e}^{-t}(\\,$\n{{field-1}}$)$"] [:db/cas 17592186321058 :question/problem "Determine the dimension of\n$\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\\}.$" "Determine the dimension of\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186321061 :question/problem "Determine the dimension of\n$\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}\\}.$" "Determine the dimension of\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186321062 :question/problem "$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}\\})=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}\\})=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186321062 :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 6 & -10\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $1$ pivot column (or alternatively, it has exactly $1$ non-zero row). \n\nTherefore, \n$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}\\})=1.$" "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 6 & -10\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $1$ pivot column (or alternatively, it has exactly $1$ non-zero row). \n\nTherefore, \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}\\})=1.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186321063 :question/problem "Determine the dimension of\n$\\text{Span}{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}}.$" "Determine the dimension of\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{Span}{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186321065 :question/problem "$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\})=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\})=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186321065 :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 5 & -6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 5 & -6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{5}{2} & 5 & -\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{5}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 5 & -6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{4} & :={R}_{4}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 5 & -6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $2$ pivot columns (or alternatively, it has exactly $2$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore, \n$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\})=2.$" "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 5 & -6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 5 & -6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{5}{2} & 5 & -\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{5}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 5 & -6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{4} & :={R}_{4}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 5 & -6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $2$ pivot columns (or alternatively, it has exactly $2$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore, \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\})=2.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186321067 :question/problem "![](images/f7/f7eebd794b43da16658750db0df3a4d4c4b894ddb80f84795ea483aa4d579f56.png)\n\nIf the graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above, then\n\n${\\int }_{-4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}." "![](images/f7/f7eebd794b43da16658750db0df3a4d4c4b894ddb80f84795ea483aa4d579f56.png)\n\nIf the graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above, then\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186321075 :question/worked-solution "To apply the ratio test we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(2x)^{n}}{(n+4)^{3/2}}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-1)^{n+2}(2x)^{n+1}}{(n+5)^{3/2}}\\cdot \\frac{(n+4)^{3/2}}{(-1)^{n+1}(2x)^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{-2x(n+4)^{3/2}}{(n+5)^{3/2}}| \\\\  & =2|x|\\cdot {lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{n+4}{n+5}\\right)}^{3/2} \\\\  & =2|x|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2|x|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied for $2|x|<1,$ or equivalently $|x|<\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, the radius of convergence is $\\frac{1}{2},$ and the series converges for any $x$ that lies in the open interval $(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nThe ratio test gives no conclusion about the convergence of the series when $L=1.$ So, when $|x|=\\frac{1}{2},$ we do not know if the series converges. This means that the endpoints $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ need to be investigated separately using another test for convergence.\n\n- When $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(2x)^{n}}{(n+4)^{3/2}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}\\cdot (1)^{n}}{(n+4)^{3/2}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{(n+4)^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{5}^{3/2}}-\\frac{1}{{6}^{3/2}}+\\frac{1}{{7}^{3/2}}-\\frac{1}{{8}^{3/2}}+⋯\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is convergent by the alternating series test.  So the endpoint $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ is included in our interval.\n\n- When $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(2x)^{n}}{(n+4)^{3/2}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(-1)^{n}}{(n+4)^{3/2}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{2n+1}}{(n+4)^{3/2}} \\\\  & =-(\\frac{1}{{5}^{3/2}}+\\frac{1}{{6}^{3/2}}+\\frac{1}{{7}^{3/2}}+\\frac{1}{{8}^{3/2}}+⋯)\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe notice that each term of this series ${a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{(n+4)^{3/2}}$ is smaller than ${b}_{n}=\\frac{1}{{n}^{3/2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{(n+4)^{3/2}}\\le \\frac{1}{{n}^{3/2}}\n$$\n\nSince ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{3/2}}$ is convergent, the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(n+4)^{3/2}}$ is convergent by the comparison test. So the endpoint $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ is included in our interval.\n\nTherefore, the interval of convergence is $[-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}],$ or equivalently, $-\\frac{1}{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{1}{2}.$" "To apply the ratio test we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(2x)^{n}}{(n+4)^{3/2}}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-1)^{n+2}(2x)^{n+1}}{(n+5)^{3/2}}\\cdot \\frac{(n+4)^{3/2}}{(-1)^{n+1}(2x)^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{-2x(n+4)^{3/2}}{(n+5)^{3/2}}| \\\\  & =2|x|\\cdot {lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{n+4}{n+5}\\right)}^{3/2} \\\\  & =2|x|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2|x|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied for $2|x|<1,$ or equivalently $|x|<\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, the radius of convergence is $\\frac{1}{2},$ and the series converges for any $x$ that lies in the open interval $(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nThe ratio test gives no conclusion about the convergence of the series when $L=1.$ So, when $|x|=\\frac{1}{2},$ we do not know if the series converges. This means that the endpoints $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ need to be investigated separately using another test for convergence.\n\n- When $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(2x)^{n}}{(n+4)^{3/2}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}\\cdot (1)^{n}}{(n+4)^{3/2}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{(n+4)^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{5}^{3/2}}-\\frac{1}{{6}^{3/2}}+\\frac{1}{{7}^{3/2}}-\\frac{1}{{8}^{3/2}}+⋯\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is convergent by the alternating series test.  So the endpoint $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ is included in our interval.\n\n- When $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(2x)^{n}}{(n+4)^{3/2}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(-1)^{n}}{(n+4)^{3/2}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{2n+1}}{(n+4)^{3/2}} \\\\  & =-(\\frac{1}{{5}^{3/2}}+\\frac{1}{{6}^{3/2}}+\\frac{1}{{7}^{3/2}}+\\frac{1}{{8}^{3/2}}+⋯)\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe notice that each term of this series ${a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{(n+4)^{3/2}}$ is smaller than ${b}_{n}=\\frac{1}{{n}^{3/2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{(n+4)^{3/2}}\\le \\frac{1}{{n}^{3/2}}\n$$\n\nSince $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{3/2}}\n$$ is convergent, the series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(n+4)^{3/2}}\n$$ is convergent by the comparison test. So the endpoint $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ is included in our interval.\n\nTherefore, the interval of convergence is $[-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}],$ or equivalently, $-\\frac{1}{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{1}{2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186321087 :question/problem "![](images/06/064e75694d82bed2177df55e2e66c3343a2c0396cd54f5d4304f952a01abb219.png)\n\nFor the polar curve $r=\\theta $ (shown above) evaluate the derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$   at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDo not round the answer.\n\n${\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =\\pi /2}=$\n{{field-1}}" "![](images/06/064e75694d82bed2177df55e2e66c3343a2c0396cd54f5d4304f952a01abb219.png)\n\nFor the polar curve $r=\\theta $ (shown above) evaluate the derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$   at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDo not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =\\pi /2}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186321091 :question/problem "![](images/c1/c1fe71fe51ae93fc2e78b52b1d3f1c270ab05d10c317345bf1440b13c570483f.png)\n\nFor the polar curve $r=30$ (shown above), evaluate the derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,{|}_{\\theta \\,=\\,3\\pi /4}=$\n{{field-1}}" "![](images/c1/c1fe71fe51ae93fc2e78b52b1d3f1c270ab05d10c317345bf1440b13c570483f.png)\n\nFor the polar curve $r=30$ (shown above), evaluate the derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,{|}_{\\theta \\,=\\,3\\pi /4}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186321110 :question/problem "![](images/e0/e0b1dc90950ba127e90fce400bc7ed1b6e8dd8e710ff880ab5949521b02a258a.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve (shown above)\n\n$$\nx=4{e}^{-t},\\,y={t}^{2}+6t,\\,0\\le t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\nfind the derivative $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at the point $P$ on the curve where $t=1.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$\\frac{dy}{dx}{|}_{t\\,=\\,1}=$  \n{{field-1}}" "![](images/e0/e0b1dc90950ba127e90fce400bc7ed1b6e8dd8e710ff880ab5949521b02a258a.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve (shown above)\n\n$$\nx=4{e}^{-t},\\,y={t}^{2}+6t,\\,0\\le t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\nfind the derivative $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at the point $P$ on the curve where $t=1.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{dy}{dx}{|}_{t\\,=\\,1}=\n$$  \n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186321111 :question/problem "![](images/18/1817c65aea37112d93f2935801a0e7dcd654706a72f729810272f48e95d39665.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve (shown above)\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,y=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi ,\n$$\n\nfind the derivative $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at the point $P$ on the curve where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$\\frac{dy}{dx}{|}_{t\\,=\\,\\pi /6}=$ \n{{field-1}}" "![](images/18/1817c65aea37112d93f2935801a0e7dcd654706a72f729810272f48e95d39665.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve (shown above)\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,y=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi ,\n$$\n\nfind the derivative $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at the point $P$ on the curve where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\d