ned}|$ only." "The determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =|\\begin{aligned}9 & 11 \\\\ 6 & -k\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the value of $x$ is given by $x=\\frac{{D}_{x}}{D},$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{x} & =|\\begin{aligned}-14 & 11 \\\\ -34 & -k\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{D}_{x}}{D} \\\\ -4 & =\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}-14 & 11 \\\\ -34 & -k\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}9 & 11 \\\\ 6 & -k\\end{aligned}|} \\\\ -4|\\begin{aligned}9 & 11 \\\\ 6 & -k\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}-14 & 11 \\\\ -34 & -k\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle |\\begin{aligned}-14 & 11 \\\\ -34 & -k\\end{aligned}|=-4|\\begin{aligned}9 & 11 \\\\ 6 & -k\\end{aligned}|\n$$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186329690 :question/problem "$|\\begin{aligned}1 & \\sqrt{2} & -1 & 10 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -3.5 & 48 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle |\\begin{aligned}1 & \\sqrt{2} & -1 & 10 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -3.5 & 48 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186329693 :question/problem "Calculate the value of \n$\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -3 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2 & -2 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 3 & 2\\end{aligned}|.$\n\n*Hint: Use elementary row operations to make the entries below ${a}_{12}$ equal to zero. You should be able to reduce the given $4\\times 4$ determinant to a $3\\times 3$ determinant.*" "Calculate the value of \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -3 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2 & -2 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 3 & 2\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\n*Hint: Use elementary row operations to make the entries below ${a}_{12}$ equal to zero. You should be able to reduce the given $4\\times 4$ determinant to a $3\\times 3$ determinant.*"] [:db/cas 17592186329694 :question/problem "$|\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\pi  & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\sqrt{5} & 1.5 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & \\pi  & 1\\end{aligned}|=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle |\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\pi  & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\sqrt{5} & 1.5 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & \\pi  & 1\\end{aligned}|=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186329698 :question/problem "Find the determinant of the matrix \n$K=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -2 & \\,-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Find the determinant of the matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle K=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -2 & \\,-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186329702 :question/worked-solution "The determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-3-10 \\\\  & -13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the value of $x$ is given by $x=\\frac{{D}_{x}}{D},$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{x} & =|\\begin{aligned}k & 5 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{D}_{x}}{D} \\\\ -1 & =\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}k & 5 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{aligned}|}{-13} \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}k & 5 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{aligned}| & =(-1)(-13) \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}k & 5 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{aligned}| & =13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is $|\\begin{aligned}k & 5 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{aligned}|=13$ only." "The determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-3-10 \\\\  & -13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the value of $x$ is given by $x=\\frac{{D}_{x}}{D},$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{x} & =|\\begin{aligned}k & 5 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{D}_{x}}{D} \\\\ -1 & =\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}k & 5 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{aligned}|}{-13} \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}k & 5 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{aligned}| & =(-1)(-13) \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}k & 5 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{aligned}| & =13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle |\\begin{aligned}k & 5 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{aligned}|=13\n$$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186329713 :question/problem "$\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta })=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta })=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186329714 :question/problem "Simplify $\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }.$" "Simplify $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186329716 :question/problem "$\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }({sin}^{3}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }({sin}^{3}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186329717 :question/problem "Simplify $\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta -1}-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta +1}.$" "Simplify $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta -1}-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta +1}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186329718 :question/problem "$\\operatorname{sin}⁡t(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t})=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{sin}⁡t(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t})=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186329722 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{36-{x}^{2}}}\\text{d}x\\,=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{36-{x}^{2}}}\\text{d}x\\,=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186329765 :question/problem "Find ${f}^{′}(2)$ if $f(x)={\\int }_{2}^{2x}\\sqrt{{t}^{3}-t}\\,\\text{d}t.$" "Find ${f}^{′}(2)$ if $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)={\\int }_{2}^{2x}\\sqrt{{t}^{3}-t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186329770 :question/worked-solution "As $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{5}{3}$ then $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{3}{5}.$\n\nLet's sketch our angle $\\theta $ in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/aa/aaba5af0542d29ef91d4b0e17907a81e32bccc402c48b32c205e7298630b5bc0.png)\n\nWe're given that $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{5}$ We can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{9}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{16}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the negative answer since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ is negative in the second quadrant. So, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{4}{5}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ and $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }$ we have:\n\n- $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}=-\\frac{4}{3}$\n\n- $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}{\\left(-\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}=-\\frac{3}{4}.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{4}{3}-\\frac{3}{4}=-\\frac{25}{12}.$" "As $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{5}{3}$ then $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nLet's sketch our angle $\\theta $ in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/aa/aaba5af0542d29ef91d4b0e17907a81e32bccc402c48b32c205e7298630b5bc0.png)\n\nWe're given that $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{5}$ We can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{9}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{16}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the negative answer since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ is negative in the second quadrant. So, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{4}{5}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\n$$ we have:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}=-\\frac{4}{3}\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}{\\left(-\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}=-\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{4}{3}-\\frac{3}{4}=-\\frac{25}{12}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186329799 :question/worked-solution "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{3}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{3},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+2x-3}{{x}^{3}-x} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{2x}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{x}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}})}{(1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{1}{x},\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}},\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}}$ and $\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}})}{(1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{0+0-0}{1-0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y=0.$" "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{3}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{3},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+2x-3}{{x}^{3}-x} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{2x}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{x}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}})}{(1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{x},\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}},\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}}\n$$ and $\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}})}{(1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{0+0-0}{1-0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y=0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186329800 :question/worked-solution "The intermediate value theorem can be applied on closed intervals on which the function is continuous. \n\nThe function $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}$ is discontinuous at the values of $x$ which make the denominator $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ equal to zero. Therefore, $f(x)$ is discontinuous at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi $, where $n$ is any integer.\n\n- On the interval $[0,1],$ we **can** apply the intermediate value theorem to $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$\n\n- On the interval $[1,2],$ we **cannot** apply the intermediate value theorem to $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ because this interval contains the point of discontinuity $\\frac{\\pi }{2}≈1.57\\,.$\n\n- On the interval $[-1,0),$ we **cannot** apply the intermediate value theorem to $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ because this interval is not closed.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $[0,1]$ only." "The intermediate value theorem can be applied on closed intervals on which the function is continuous. \n\nThe function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\n$$ is discontinuous at the values of $x$ which make the denominator $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ equal to zero. Therefore, $f(x)$ is discontinuous at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi $, where $n$ is any integer.\n\n- On the interval $[0,1],$ we **can** apply the intermediate value theorem to $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$\n\n- On the interval $[1,2],$ we **cannot** apply the intermediate value theorem to $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ because this interval contains the point of discontinuity $\\frac{\\pi }{2}≈1.57\\,.$\n\n- On the interval $[-1,0),$ we **cannot** apply the intermediate value theorem to $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ because this interval is not closed.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $[0,1]$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186329807 :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a  function such that ${\\int }_{5}^{12}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=12$ and ${\\int }_{5}^{-5}6\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=18.$ What is the value of ${\\int }_{-5}^{12}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x?$" "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a  function such that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{5}^{12}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=12\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{5}^{-5}6\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=18.\n$$ What is the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-5}^{12}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186329808 :question/problem "Let $f$ and $g$ be continuous functions such that ${\\int }_{0}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=18,{\\int }_{0}^{7}\\frac{1}{2}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x=5,$ and ${\\int }_{3}^{7}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x=4.$ What is the value of ${\\int }_{0}^{3}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x?$" "Let $f$ and $g$ be continuous functions such that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=18,{\\int }_{0}^{7}\\frac{1}{2}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x=5,\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{3}^{7}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x=4.\n$$ What is the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{3}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186329808 :question/worked-solution "Let's simplify one of the given integrals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{7}\\frac{1}{2}\\,g(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =5 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{7}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =5 \\\\ {\\int }_{0}^{7}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the adjacent intervals rule and substituting the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{7}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{3}^{7}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x \\\\ {\\int }_{0}^{7}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the sum rule for definite integrals and substituting the known values, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{7}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x+4 \\\\ {\\int }_{0}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{0}^{7}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x+4 \\\\ 18-10 & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x+4 \\\\ 4 & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${\\int }_{0}^{3}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x=4.$" "Let's simplify one of the given integrals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{7}\\frac{1}{2}\\,g(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =5 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{7}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =5 \\\\ {\\int }_{0}^{7}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the adjacent intervals rule and substituting the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{7}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{3}^{7}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x \\\\ {\\int }_{0}^{7}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the sum rule for definite integrals and substituting the known values, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{7}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x+4 \\\\ {\\int }_{0}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{0}^{7}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x+4 \\\\ 18-10 & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x+4 \\\\ 4 & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{3}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x=4.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186329820 :question/worked-solution "The invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\text{Col}(A) &={R}^{n} \\\\ \\text{dim(Col}(A)) &=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\ \\text{Null}(A) &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) &=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the options separately.\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $det(A)=0,$ then $A$ has no inverse.* $\\,✓$  \n\nIndeed,  a matrix $A$ is invertible if and only if $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $det(A)=0,$ then $\\text{rank}(A)=n.$*$\\,\\times$\n\nSince our matrix is not invertible, according to the invertible matrix theorem, it must be $\\text{rank}(A)\\ne n.$\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $det(A)=0,$ then all columns of $A$ are pivot columns.*  $\\,\\times$\n\nSince $\\text{rank}(A)\\ne n$, it must be $\\text{dim(Col}(A))<n,$ which means that not all columns of $A$ are pivot columns.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$A$ has no inverse” only." "The invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\text{Col}(A) &={R}^{n} \\\\ \\text{dim(Col}(A)) &=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\ \\text{Null}(A) &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) &=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the options separately.\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $det(A)=0,$ then $A$ has no inverse.* $\\,✓$  \n\nIndeed,  a matrix $A$ is invertible if and only if $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $det(A)=0,$ then $\\text{rank}(A)=n.$*$\\,\\times$\n\nSince our matrix is not invertible, according to the invertible matrix theorem, it must be $\\text{rank}(A)\\ne n.$\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $det(A)=0,$ then all columns of $A$ are pivot columns.*  $\\,\\times$\n\nSince $\\text{rank}(A)\\ne n$, it must be $\\text{dim(Col}(A))<n,$ which means that not all columns of $A$ are pivot columns.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$A$ has no inverse” only."] [:db/cas 17592186329832 :question/problem "For $x>0,$ find ${f}^{′}(x)$ if $f(x)={\\int }_{-{x}^{2}}^{-4}\\frac{1}{t}\\,\\text{d}t.$" "For $x>0,$ find ${f}^{′}(x)$ if $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)={\\int }_{-{x}^{2}}^{-4}\\frac{1}{t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186329836 :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a  function such that ${\\int }_{2}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=9$ and ${\\int }_{9}^{6}4\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=20.$ What is the value of ${\\int }_{2}^{9}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x?$" "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a  function such 