_{2}^{-2}\\frac{1}{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-3\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{2}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=10.\n$$  What is the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-2}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186329893 :question/worked-solution "Let $r$  be the radius, $A$ the area, and $C$ the circumference of the circle. We wish to find $r$ at the moment when $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{2}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nTo start, let's set up an equation relating the area $A$ and the circumference $C$ of the circle. Since $C=2\\pi r$ and $A=\\pi {r}^{2},$ the relationship between $A$ and $C$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2}=\\pi {\\left(\\frac{C}{2\\pi }\\right)}^{2}=\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}C}\\left(\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{C}{2\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{2}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{C}{2\\pi } \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & =\\frac{C}{2\\pi } \\\\ C & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, since $C=2\\pi r,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi r & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3} \\\\ r & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $r$  be the radius, $A$ the area, and $C$ the circumference of the circle. We wish to find $r$ at the moment when $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{2}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nTo start, let's set up an equation relating the area $A$ and the circumference $C$ of the circle. Since $C=2\\pi r$ and $A=\\pi {r}^{2},$ the relationship between $A$ and $C$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2}=\\pi {\\left(\\frac{C}{2\\pi }\\right)}^{2}=\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}C}\\left(\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{C}{2\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{2}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t},\n$$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{C}{2\\pi } \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & =\\frac{C}{2\\pi } \\\\ C & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, since $C=2\\pi r,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi r & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3} \\\\ r & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186329894 :question/worked-solution "Let $r$  be the radius, $A$ the area, and $D$ the diameter of the circle. We wish to find $r$ at the moment when $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{1}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nTo start, let's set up an equation relating the area $A$ and the diameter $D$ of the circle. Since $D=2r$ and $A=\\pi {r}^{2},$ the relationship between $A$ and $D$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2}=\\pi {\\left(\\frac{D}{2}\\right)}^{2}=\\frac{\\pi {D}^{2}}{4}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{\\pi {D}^{2}}{4}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}D}\\left(\\frac{\\pi {D}^{2}}{4}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi D}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{1}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi D}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & =\\frac{\\pi D}{2} \\\\ D & =\\frac{2}{3\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, since $D=2r,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2r & =\\frac{2}{3\\pi } \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{3\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $r$  be the radius, $A$ the area, and $D$ the diameter of the circle. We wish to find $r$ at the moment when $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{1}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nTo start, let's set up an equation relating the area $A$ and the diameter $D$ of the circle. Since $D=2r$ and $A=\\pi {r}^{2},$ the relationship between $A$ and $D$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2}=\\pi {\\left(\\frac{D}{2}\\right)}^{2}=\\frac{\\pi {D}^{2}}{4}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{\\pi {D}^{2}}{4}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}D}\\left(\\frac{\\pi {D}^{2}}{4}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi D}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{1}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t},\n$$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi D}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & =\\frac{\\pi D}{2} \\\\ D & =\\frac{2}{3\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, since $D=2r,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2r & =\\frac{2}{3\\pi } \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{3\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186329930 :question/problem "For the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x),$ you are given the following data:\n\n$$\nf(1)=8,\\,{f}^{′}(1)=6,\\,g(1)=4,\\,{g}^{′}(1)=4\n$$\n\nCompute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\\right)$ at $x=1.$" "For the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x),$ you are given the following data:\n\n$$\nf(1)=8,\\,{f}^{′}(1)=6,\\,g(1)=4,\\,{g}^{′}(1)=4\n$$\n\nCompute $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\\right)\n$$ at $x=1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186329944 :question/problem "The values of functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ and their derivatives at $x=3$ are given in the table below. \n\n| $x$ | $f(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $2$ | $-1$ | $-4$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n\nCompute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\\right)$ at $x=3.$" "The values of functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ and their derivatives at $x=3$ are given in the table below. \n\n| $x$ | $f(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $2$ | $-1$ | $-4$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n\nCompute $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\\right)\n$$ at $x=3.$"] [:db/cas 17592186332661 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int csc⁡x(3cot⁡x+2csc⁡x)\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int csc⁡x(3cot⁡x+2csc⁡x)\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332662 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int sec⁡x(7sec⁡x+4tan⁡x)\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int sec⁡x(7sec⁡x+4tan⁡x)\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332663 :question/problem "$\\int (\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}-6{x}^{2})\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}-6{x}^{2})\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332664 :question/problem "$\\int (\\frac{4}{1+{x}^{2}}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (\\frac{4}{1+{x}^{2}}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332665 :question/problem "$\\int (\\frac{6}{1+{x}^{2}}-9)\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (\\frac{6}{1+{x}^{2}}-9)\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332667 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}-7}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{{x}^{2}-7}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332668 :question/problem "![](images/96/96622ea1476cf79ec3ff6d6486d77eda465de80d005304f542b4aadfb6be2b7a.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of two line segments. Find the value of ${\\int }_{-3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" "![](images/96/96622ea1476cf79ec3ff6d6486d77eda465de80d005304f542b4aadfb6be2b7a.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of two line segments. Find the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332669 :question/problem "![](images/55/555b702c1903f8cc4e379ad4281aa86708757755e4f9fb14b0ec7790a80a17dd.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y=f(x)$ above, find the value of \n${\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" "![](images/55/555b702c1903f8cc4e379ad4281aa86708757755e4f9fb14b0ec7790a80a17dd.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y=f(x)$ above, find the value of \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332671 :question/problem "What is ${\\int }_{2}^{6}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a left Riemann sum?" "What is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{2}^{6}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ expressed as the limit of a left Riemann sum?"] [:db/cas 17592186332672 :question/problem "What is ${\\int }_{3}^{4}5{x}^{2}\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?" "What is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{3}^{4}5{x}^{2}\\text{d}x\n$$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?"] [:db/cas 17592186332673 :question/problem "![](images/90/90ab7f470adc629be8c089cb74a259b9663c463cf224251b099cae33044b1ac5.png)\n\nConsider the graph of the function $y=f(x),$ shown above. It consists of a line segment and an arc of a quarter-circle centered at $(0,0)$ with radius $2.$ Find the value of ${\\int }_{-3}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" "![](images/90/90ab7f470adc629be8c089cb74a259b9663c463cf224